    "doi": "https://doi.org/10.55363/idmc.fhxo2779",
    "abstract": "Nigeria faces high levels of disaster displacement. Between 2008 and 2024, the country recorded more than 9.9 million disaster displacements, the highest figure in Africa. Climate change is expected to intensify these risks under both optimistic and pessimistic climate change scenarios. Using IDMC\u2019s updated Global Displacement Risk Model, the report estimates the likelihood and scale of future displacement under different climate change scenarios. The short risk profile synthesises information on hazard intensity, population vulnerability and exposure to identify displacement hotspots, key drivers and potential displacement impacts in Nigeria. Together, these provide a practical entry point for governments and partners to prioritise prevention and inform risk reduction.",
    "field": "Disaster displacement risk",
    "problem": "Existing approaches to disaster displacement risk are inadequate because they rely on historical trends, failing to incorporate forward-looking probabilistic models that account for evolving climate scenarios and socioeconomic vulnerabilities.",
    "method": "This paper proposes a probabilistic multi-hazard risk model to estimate future displacement by assessing the likelihood of severe housing damage and livelihood loss from riverine floods, storm surges, and droughts under different climate scenarios.",
    "task": "The task is to assess the future risk of displacement in Nigeria from riverine floods, storm surges, and droughts using a probabilistic risk model that incorporates different climate change scenarios.",
