        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "cyclonic winds",
            "source_entity_type": "Natural_Hazard",
            "relation": "AFFECTED_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "Nigeria",
            "target_entity_type": "Country",
            "paragraph": "Our study outlines an effort to create and apply a multi-hazards displacement risk model that utilizes novel methods for vulnerability assessment. The methodology gauges the potential displacement of individuals due to riverine floods, storm surges, cyclonic winds and drought.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": false,
            "eval": false
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "riverine floods",
            "source_entity_type": "Natural_Hazard",
            "relation": "IS_OF_TYPE",
            "target_entity_instance": "Flood",
            "target_entity_type": "NaturalHazardType",
            "paragraph": "Riverine floods\nThe analysis of flood displacement risk at a country level shows an Annual Average Displacement (AAD) value of around 540,000 people under current climate conditions.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "storm surges",
            "source_entity_type": "Natural_Hazard",
            "relation": "IS_OF_TYPE",
            "target_entity_instance": "Storm Surge",
            "target_entity_type": "NaturalHazardType",
            "paragraph": "Storm surges\nStorm surge is the abnormal rise in seawater level during a storm. The surge is caused primarily by a storm\u2019s winds pushing water onshore. The amplitude of the storm surge at any given location depends on the orientation of the coastline with the storm track; the intensity, size, and speed of the storm; and the local bathymetry. 14 Our model assesses the risk from storm surges, taking into account how sea-level rise in a changing climate could affect potential run-up in coastal areas.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": false
        },
        {
